### name: lex.ident.table-and-column
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"TOTAL": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "total", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
TOTAL
--- expect
`o`.`total`
===
### name: lex.ident.no-table
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"TOTAL": {"kind": "column", "column": "total", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
TOTAL
--- expect
`total`
===
### name: lex.ident.quote-inside-a-name-is-escaped
--- note
A backtick in a column name must not end the identifier early.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ODD": {"kind": "column", "table": "a`b", "column": "c`d", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
ODD
--- expect
`a``b`.`c``d`
===
### name: lex.ident.raw-bypasses-quoting
--- note
The one place a caller can inject arbitrary SQL, spelled distinctly so it is
visible in a review.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"NOW": {"kind": "column", "raw": "CURRENT_TIMESTAMP", "type": "UNKNOWN"}}
--- source
NOW
--- expect
CURRENT_TIMESTAMP
### name: lex.number.long-numeral-reaches-sql
--- note
The SQL layer parses numerals too -- Emit calls Dec::parse and re-renders
Dec::format's output rather than the caller's characters -- so a host limit on
numeral length is not an evaluator-only concern. On the Python host this program
raised a ValueError out of compile(), before the translator ran at all, while
PHP produced the SQL. spec/SPEC.md 6.4 sets the length a numeral may have; both
hosts must reach that answer, and neither may reach it by crashing.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source

--- expect

===
